<description>
Restore the black-erased cells by tiling the smallest repeating pattern visible in the remaining cells.

Input structure
A same-size grid contains an otherwise regular two-dimensional color lattice, with black rectangles masking some cells.
Rule
Treat black pixels as unknown, infer the unique smallest rectangular period that agrees with every non-black pixel, and tile it across the full grid.
Key insight
The visible cells still include every residue of the repeating tile, so the black pixels are occlusions rather than objects to preserve.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "the visible non-black cells of the repeating two-dimensional color lattice",
  "transform": "treat black pixels as occlusions; infer the unique smallest rectangular repeating tile consistent with the visible cells and tile it over the full grid"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 30x30 [observed]
  tile:
    shape: 2 rows x W cols, W in 2-8
    row_0: all bg color
    row_1: [bg, perm of (W-1) distinct non-zero non-bg colors]
    bg_color: 1 [observed]; solver requires bg != 0
  output: tile tiled modularly across the full grid (tile need not divide grid size)
  occlusions:
    count: 5 solid axis-aligned black (color 0) rectangles
    dimensions: 2-7 rows x 2-7 cols per rectangle
    placement: scattered; may touch edges; two rects may share an edge forming an L-shape connected component
    total_coverage: 5-10% of grid
  input: output with occlusion rectangles replaced by 0
  constraint: every tile residue (r%2, c%W) must remain visible (non-black) somewhere in the input, ensuring unique tile recovery

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick W in 2-8. Choose bg color (non-zero). Choose (W-1) distinct non-zero non-bg colors; shuffle for tile row 1.
    2. Build 2xW tile; tile it modularly across 30x30 grid to get the output.
    3. Place 5 non-overlapping solid black rectangles (each 2-7 rows x 2-7 cols) at random positions.
    4. Validate every tile residue (r%2, c%W) has at least one uncovered cell; if not, retry rectangle placement.
    5. Zero out rectangle cells in the output to produce the input.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

